{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# fsck should RESTORE, not just refuse\n\nChange 1557 (bug b636) makes the snapshot rebuild FAIL CLOSED when the prior SNAPSHOT cites\nsource events absent from disk. That stops new corruption, but leaves the existing population\npermanently un-repairable: a dry run of the fixed fsck reports 505 live tickets in exactly\nthat shape, and `fsck --repair` can now never drive them to fsck-zero.\n\n## Evidence that restoration is possible\nThe deleted event files are still in `origin/tickets` history (compaction deleted them from\nthe worktree; the blobs remain reachable at the deleting commit's parent). Measured over the\n505 flagged tickets: 504 have deletion records covering every cited-missing uuid, 1 partial,\n0 with no record at all. Validated end-to-end on a COPY of the live store: restoring every\ndeleted event file then rebuilding restored 214/214 already-regressed tickets to their\ncorrect status (486 events recovered).\n\n## Prior art to REUSE (not re-derive)\n`attest/authorship.py:build_ticket_position_commit_map` already implements the ticket-scoped,\ndirectory-prefix, SINGLE-PASS `git log --diff-filter=A --name-only` walk and is cost-tuned for\nit (the batched form of the per-event `resolve_event_commit`). This work needs the identical\nshape with `--diff-filter=D` to map deleted-path -> deleting-commit. Reuse that helper's\nstructure (and extract the shared walk if it is cleanly parameterizable on the filter) rather\nthan writing a second history-walk. Blob reads use the existing `git show <ref>:<path>` idiom\nalready used in `binding_store.py` and `_store/compat.py`.\n\n## Naming: avoid the overloaded 'recover'\n`_commands/fsck_recover.py` already owns 'recover' in this subsystem. This work uses\n`restore_deleted_sources` for the new primitive so the two are not confused.\n\n## Why the snapshot's citation list is not enough\nTicket 72d5 recovered its close STATUS but still read in_progress: an EARLIER deleted STATUS\nthat no surviving snapshot cites broke the chain's current_status precondition, so the reducer\nrejected both it and the close. Restoration must sweep the ticket's full deletion history,\nNOT the newest snapshot's source_event_uuids.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] `compact_rebuild.restore_deleted_sources(tracker, ticket_id, ticket_dir) -> list[str]`\n      restores every event file deleted from that ticket dir in tickets-branch history,\n      enumerated by ONE ticket-scoped `git log --diff-filter=D --name-only -- <tid>/` pass\n      modelled on `attest.authorship.build_ticket_position_commit_map`, each blob read at the\n      deleting commit's parent, written back as a folded `.retired` source (never resurrected\n      as a live event). Returns the restored filenames.\n- [ ] `compact_rebuild.rebuild_with_restore(tracker, ticket_id, ticket_dir)` OWNS the\n      restore -> re-check `snapshot_missing_sources` -> rebuild decision and is the single\n      entrypoint `fsck._maybe_repair_snapshot` calls on the repair path. It refuses (b636\n      fail-closed) only when restoration cannot complete the log.\n- [ ] `--dry-run` reports per ticket how many events WOULD be restored and whether a rebuild\n      would follow, writing nothing.\n- [ ] Restoration is per ticket (no full-history index build) so it stays usable on a large\n      store.\n- [ ] Held-out test: a ticket whose close exists ONLY in history is restored and reads closed\n      again after `fsck --repair`.\n- [ ] Held-out test: a ticket with genuinely unrecoverable sources still refuses and is\n      surfaced, unchanged from 1557."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786260731218106001,"uuid":"6ba8e3a7-2af8-4223-a777-f53d1736558c"}